{
  "dom_id": "step-q36223",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the radius of the osculating circle at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$,  given that the curve and its unit tangent $T(t)$ are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,1⟩,\\,T(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩\\,.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of the osculating circle: \n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the radius of the osculating circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
